{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>The breast to wave it as a wave-offering before YHWH.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): he would wave the breast upon the portions of fat but not the thigh, since concerning the thigh it is said ״ואת שוק הימין תתנו תרומה לכהן״, and it does not mention waving concerning it." "<b>And indeed</b> we find explicitly written: ״the thigh of the heave-offering and the breast of the wave-offering upon the fats of the fire-offerings״ — from which it appears that the thigh and the breast were waved together upon the fats, and after the waving he would burn the fats upon the altar, as it is written ״והקטיר הכהן את החלב המזבחה״, and the thigh and the breast were given to the priests, as it is written ״והיה החזה לאהרן ולבניו״, and it is written ״ואת שוק הימין תתנו תרומה לכהן״. And this is how this waving was done: he would place the fats by the hand of the owners, with the breast and thigh above them, and wave them before the altar. And this waving of the breast and thigh is like the motion of the lulav — extending and drawing back, raising and lowering: extending and drawing back to Him who owns the four directions of the world, raising and lowering to Him who owns the heavens and the earth." "<b>And it seems</b> to me that the reason these two limbs — the breast and the thigh — were singled out for waving upon the altar is that they are the cause of motion in man and likewise in other living creatures: for the heart, which is in motion, is located in the place of the breast, and the motion of every living creature is likewise in the thighs. Therefore this commandment in the offerings requires that these two limbs, which are the possessors of motion, be brought before the altar, to give thanks and praise thereby to the Master of all, who sustains the entire world through the motion of the spheres — and this is what they said: to Him who owns the four directions of the world, and to Him who owns the heavens and the earth." "<b>And further</b> one may say concerning the matter of extending and drawing back, raising and lowering — whether in the motion of the breast and thigh or in the motion of the lulav — that therein is an allusion to exile and redemption: extending, after ״וילכו בלא כח לפני רודף״ (Lamentations 1:6); and drawing back, after ״הנני מביא אותם מארץ צפון״ (Jeremiah 31:7). Raising, to Him who raised His Shekhinah from the midst of Israel, as it is said ״ויעל כבוד ה' מעל תוך העיר״ (Ezekiel 11:23). And lowering, to Him who is destined to bring it down and cause it to dwell in Israel, as it is said ״כן ירד ה' צבאות לצבוא על הר ציון״ (Isaiah 31:4)." "<b>And there is</b> in it yet another allusion, to the revival of the dead: extending, after what is written ״רוח הולך״ (Psalms 78:39); and drawing back, after what is written ״ותבא בהם הרוח ויחיו״ (Ezekiel 37:10); raising, after what is written ״והרוח תשוב אל האלהים אשר נתנה״ (Ecclesiastes 12:7); and lowering, after what is written ״ופחי בהרוגים האלה ויחיו״ (Ezekiel 37:9)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:23:54.764227890Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}